<!--
    A byte-masked compact jump table that is its function's last, assembled
    from decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/switchnext_aarch64.s
    (the .text and .rodata of switchnext_aarch64).

    `name_of` indexes tab_names (3 halfwords at 0x110dc) with `x0 & 0xff`,
    so the basic model reads 256 rows. The rows past its three are
    next_kind's table (0x110e2) and the zero bytes after it, measured from
    name_of's base: 0x18 lands in next_kind, 0 back on name_of's first
    case, so the run-on cut keeps every row. With switchextent (default on)
    the table ends where next_kind's table, the next function's, begins:
    three cases. Off, the rows from next_kind's table print as phantom cases
    returning next_kind's 0x28.

    `last_two` (0x20008, after `first_fn` at 0x20000) is the last known
    function: its own `x0 & 0xff` table tab_a (signed halfwords, `ldrsh`,
    at 0x2107c) is followed by a range-checked table and then by negative
    rows, which reach back into first_fn, so its sanity check asks the flow
    for the next functions' tables. None follows, and the flow still runs
    the recovery once more: last_two stays a switch, not one computed call.
    The extent scan names both of last_two's tables, so tab_a ends at tab_b
    (three rows, its third the second dispatch) and tab_b's four range-checked
    cases print as the second switch.
    Assembled with llvm-mc -triple=aarch64 and ld.lld -Ttext=0x20000:
        first_fn: mov w0, #7; ret
        last_two: and x8, x0, #0xff; adrp x9, tab_a; add x9, x9, :lo12:tab_a
                  adr x10, La; ldrsh w11, [x9, x8, lsl #1]
                  add x10, x10, w11, sxtw #2; br x10
        La:  mov w0, #50; ret
        La1: mov w0, #51; ret
        La2: cmp w1, #3; b.hi Ld; adrp x9, tab_b; add x9, x9, :lo12:tab_b
             adr x10, Lb; ldrh w11, [x9, w1, uxtw #1]
             add x10, x10, x11, lsl #2; br x10
        Lb .. Lb3: mov w0, #60 .. #63; ret      Ld: mov w0, #-1; ret
        tab_a: .hword 0, 2, 4
        tab_b: .hword 0, 2, 4, 6, 0xfff8, 0xfffa, 0xfff9, 0xfffb; .space 512

    `disp` (0x8000) indexes tab1 (0x9078) with all of w0 and no range check,
    so switchextent ends tab1 at disp's next table, tab2, three rows. Its
    row 2 is `inner` (0x8068), the known function after disp, so the
    recovery bounded that way asks for the next functions' tables in turn,
    and the flow answers that too: disp keeps both switches. Assembled with
    llvm-mc -triple=aarch64 and ld.lld -Ttext=0x8000:
        disp: adrp x9, tab1; add x9, x9, :lo12:tab1; adr x10, L1
              ldrh w11, [x9, w0, uxtw #1]; add x10, x10, x11, lsl #2; br x10
        L1:  mov w0, #10; ret
        L1a: mov w0, #11; b L2s
        L2s: cmp w1, #2; b.hi L2d; adrp x9, tab2; add x9, x9, :lo12:tab2
             adr x10, L2; ldrh w11, [x9, w1, uxtw #1]
             add x10, x10, x11, lsl #2; br x10
        L2 .. L2b: mov w0, #20 .. #22; ret      L2d: mov w0, #-1; ret
        inner: mov w0, #12; ret                 after: mov w0, #99; ret
        tab1: .hword 0, 2, (inner - L1) / 4
        tab2: .hword 0, 2, 4; .space 64
-->
